      en: "Alédjo Wildlife Reserve, located in central Togo's Sotouboua Prefecture, encompasses 30 km² of diverse habitats including dry forest, savanna, and rocky outcrops. It is home to troops of olive baboons, warthogs, and several antelope species. The reserve borders the Alédjo Fault, a prominent geological feature. Established in 1952, it was originally managed for trophy hunting, but now focuses on conservation. Birdlife is abundant, with species such as the red-cheeked cordon-bleu and grey-headed kingfisher. The reserve has limited tourist infrastructure but offers opportunities for hiking and wildlife viewing. Local communities participate in management through collaborative agreements.",
